On Thu, 17 Dec 2003, Kenneth Call wrote:
> How are tiles handled? I am trying to add my own monster and compile
> the tiled version for winnt, but since I didn't add my own tile to, I
> think, tiles.bmp all of the tiles at my monster and beyond are
> shifted. Is there an easier way to add a tile than to shift all of
> them over and down? How do they work? In tiles.bmp I see graphics for
> what I believe is the shimmering dragon and baby, shimmering dragon
> scales, and shimmering dragon scale mail. How does the program know to
> skip over these tiles when the code for the shimmering dragon has been
> surrounded by #ifdef 0.

I don't know about a single tiles.bmp file (because I don't use tiles).
If this is something the game creates with 16x16 pixel tiles with a 16
color palatte, then you need to add tiles to monsters.txt, objects.txt,
and other.txt, all found in win/share. They should be in the same order as
in monst.c and objects.c. If you use photoshop, then I have an empty tile
and palatte that makes tiling much easier (If you save the image in raw
format, it indents and newlines for you.

If you are using precompiler conditionals (and you should be) you will
need to edit win/share/tilemap.c.  The struct you need to change will be
conditionals, around line 31. An entry for conditionals goes like this:
{ glyph_type, preceding PM/obj, "name of your monst/object" }
Make sure to surround with negated precompiler conditionals (ifndef
instead of ifdef). There's probably more that I don't know about, so make
sure to test everything.

If you're making 32x32 pixel tiles, then I think there is a DIY kit
avaliable, but I haven't tried it out. You can find it at:
http://www.geocities.co.jp/SiliconValley-SanJose/9606/nh/eng.html
the link is near the bottom of the page.

